WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.235
Back to the corner shop.

2
00:00:04.235 --> 00:00:08.740
Unpacking every box each morning is silly when the stock hardly changes.

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:15.809
So a sensible shopkeeper leaves yesterday's filled shelves in the back room, and wheels them out at opening time.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.884
That's the archive from earlier in this lesson.

5
00:00:19.884 --> 00:00:27.653
The JDK ships one for its own classes, and AppCDS, application class data sharing, adds yours.

6
00:00:27.653 --> 00:00:34.513
On the lesson's archive ladder, a small program loaded 909 classes with no sharing.

7
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:38.149
With AppCDS, 794 of its 826 came ready made.

8
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.825
So here's a question you might be shy to ask.

9
00:00:41.825 --> 00:00:45.688
The classes are already on the shelves, so what's left to save?

10
00:00:45.688 --> 00:00:48.324
Why yet another thing, called an AOT cache?

11
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.724
Because full shelves are not yet a shop that runs itself.

12
00:00:52.724 --> 00:00:55.756
A class taken from the shelf still has to be linked.

13
00:00:55.756 --> 00:00:58.089
Every lambda is still built on the spot.

14
00:00:58.089 --> 00:01:00.947
And the JIT still learns everything from scratch.

15
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:04.442
Linking sounds grand, so let's make it small.

16
00:01:04.442 --> 00:01:09.001
A class file names other code by text, like java/util/Map.get.

17
00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:17.089
The first time an instruction runs, the JVM looks that name up, and swaps it for a pointer to the real method.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.410
In the shop, a customer asks for tea, and the shopkeeper checks the floor plan to find the right shelf.

19
00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.842
The tea is right there.

20
00:01:25.842 --> 00:01:29.327
She still has to find it, the first time, every morning.

21
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:30.788
A lambda costs more.

22
00:01:30.788 --> 00:01:38.781
Its first run builds a small hidden class on the spot, like a gift basket put together the first time someone orders one.

23
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:41.420
Wouldn't it be nice if she kept notes?

24
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.532
Tea, shelf three.

25
00:01:42.532 --> 00:01:44.364
Gift basket, already packed.

26
00:01:44.364 --> 00:01:46.981
Then tomorrow would start further along.

27
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:51.521
A clever shopkeeper does exactly that, in three phases.

28
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:52.209
Training.

29
00:01:52.209 --> 00:01:55.821
An ordinary day in the shop, with a notebook on the counter.

30
00:01:55.821 --> 00:02:00.156
She writes down which shelves she used, and which shortcuts she learned.

31
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:00.844
Assembly.

32
00:02:00.844 --> 00:02:05.336
In the evening the shop is closed, and she writes the notes up neatly.

33
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:06.203
Production.

34
00:02:06.203 --> 00:02:10.152
Every morning after that, she opens with the notes in hand.

35
00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:11.150
Let's map it.

36
00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:22.159
The ordinary day is a training run of your program on realistic work, while the JVM writes what it loaded and linked into an AOT configuration file: the notebook.

37
00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:26.576
Assembly turns that into the cache, without running your program.

38
00:02:26.576 --> 00:02:28.683
Production is every real start.

39
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:37.172
On JDK 25, one command does training and assembly together, and every production start names the cache.

40
00:02:37.172 --> 00:02:43.715
The two step form from JDK 24 still works, for when the phases happen at different times.

41
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.598
AOT stands for ahead of time, which is a grand name for something very ordinary.

42
00:02:49.598 --> 00:02:51.909
The shop did its homework last night.

43
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:53.363
So the point is this.

44
00:02:53.363 --> 00:02:56.389
The work moves from every morning to one evening.

45
00:02:57.267 --> 00:02:58.883
So what's in the cache?

46
00:02:58.883 --> 00:03:01.271
Classes already loaded and linked.

47
00:03:01.271 --> 00:03:04.433
Names and lambda call sites already resolved.

48
00:03:04.433 --> 00:03:10.474
Java objects the JVM would otherwise build at every start, such as its map of modules.

49
00:03:10.474 --> 00:03:16.095
And on JDK 25, the method profiles from training, which tell the JIT what's hot.

50
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.355
Now a prediction.

51
00:03:18.355 --> 00:03:20.268
Here's the ladder again.

52
00:03:20.268 --> 00:03:23.775
With AppCDS, the program loaded 826 classes.

53
00:03:23.775 --> 00:03:28.238
With the AOT cache, will it load more classes, or fewer?

54
00:03:28.238 --> 00:03:29.513
Pause and guess.

55
00:03:32.733 --> 00:03:33.246
More.

56
00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:36.216
959, more than any other row.

57
00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:39.495
And all 959 came from the cache.

58
00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:42.061
Well, that's not what fewer looks like.

59
00:03:42.061 --> 00:03:49.243
But it's the good kind of more: the JVM loads and links every cached class before main runs, straight from the cache.

60
00:03:49.243 --> 00:03:53.724
She wheels out every shelf she used yesterday, before unlocking the door.

61
00:03:53.867 --> 00:04:07.734
On that one run, on a busy machine, the wall clock went from 388 milliseconds with no sharing, to 187 with the default archive, 153 with AppCDS, and 86 with the cache.

62
00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:11.139
It's one run, so treat it as a direction.

63
00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:18.135
The research behind this course measured more carefully, on JDK 25.

64
00:04:18.135 --> 00:04:30.180
For a 300 class app, start-up CPU time fell from 504 milliseconds to 81, wall time from 431 to 98, and peak memory from 64 MiB to 42.

65
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.079
None of it needed a code change.

66
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:41.370
This is Project Leyden, the OpenJDK project that moves start-up work out of production runs.

67
00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:50.342
The AOT cache is its first result: it arrived in JDK 24, and JDK 25 added the one step command and the profiles.

68
00:04:50.342 --> 00:04:52.985
They're final, standard features.

69
00:04:53.867 --> 00:04:55.017
Now the guardrail.

70
00:04:55.017 --> 00:04:57.956
Suppose she takes her notes on a quiet Sunday.

71
00:04:57.956 --> 00:05:00.000
The notes say nobody buys bread.

72
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.451
So on Monday morning, the bread is still in its boxes.

73
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.615
That's the rule.

74
00:05:04.615 --> 00:05:08.167
Code the training never ran gets nothing from the cache.

75
00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:09.625
It loads the usual way.

76
00:05:09.625 --> 00:05:12.416
So train on work that looks like production.

77
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:15.258
And the notes fit one shop.

78
00:05:15.258 --> 00:05:21.317
A cache belongs to one class path, one JDK release, and one operating system and CPU.

79
00:05:21.317 --> 00:05:24.809
Change any of them, and you make the cache again.

80
00:05:25.733 --> 00:05:29.252
So, what's left to save once the classes are on the shelves?

81
00:05:29.252 --> 00:05:33.767
The linking, the lambdas, the start-up objects, and a head start for the JIT.

82
00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:38.809
Train on a real day, assemble in the evening, and every morning starts from the notes.

83
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:39.739
Quick check.

84
00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:44.847
Your training run only started the service and stopped it, with no requests at all.

85
00:05:44.847 --> 00:05:47.617
Which code gets the full help from the cache?

86
00:05:50.867 --> 00:05:52.379
Only the start-up code.

87
00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:58.496
The request code never ran in training, so it isn't in the notes, and it loads the usual way.
